{
  "packet_id": "f6881829b551a19a",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 keeps the objective (is there evidence to justify investment) separate from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives, including B2B expansion, no expansion and a change of route to market. It misses a constraint the dossier signals: SYN-AQP-UPDATE refers to dossier evidence on insolvency and financing, and aqp-fy2024-drinking-water-drop shows a 55% fall in Drinking Water revenue. It also does not note that Aquaporin reaches consumers through OEM partners (Vestel, Philips/AquaShield), not as a consumer brand. The €20,000 budget and 12-week limit have no source in the case packet."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 (A9) says who changes behaviour (health-conscious bottled-water users) and why (taste and safety concerns are removed). The chain does not reach value this manufacturer captures. 'Premium pricing for a differentiated unit, subscription services' assumes Aquaporin sells finished units, but the dossier shows it supplies membranes to OEMs (aqp-vestel-rafine-pro-2024, aqp-philips-china-supply-2023). The 'clear health benefits' are asserted, not sourced."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 (premium health/taste) and T2 (convenience/low maintenance) are rival explanations of adoption with somewhat different product emphasis. They overlap heavily: T1 explicitly includes 'convenience'. Both assume the same direct-to-consumer purifier and customer, so they do not imply different channels or customers."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9"],
      "reason": "T1 has an observable prediction ('willingness to pay at least 20% more'). Others are vague, such as 'higher conversion rates than standard membranes' when consumers do not buy membranes directly. The run never says which failed prediction would sink T1, and the 20% threshold is an unjustified assumption."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12"],
      "reason": "Only sources that agree are assessed. A11 and A12 do state what each source does not show, and A12 labels the ONE text a company assertion. The Flanders study is not checked for place or fit with the proposed Danish sample. Contrary or complicating sources are never assessed: aqp-srilanka-filter-disuse (24% non-use), aqp-dwd-recital40-confidence (policy pushes tap-water confidence), aqp-fy2024-drinking-water-drop, and the Coway rental data that bear on the maintenance model."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A13"],
      "reason": "A13 searches against T1 and retrieves the Sri Lanka disuse study and the DWD recital, but reports nothing about what they show. No challenge to T2 is attempted. The search is a mechanical gesture without a fair report of its results."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A14"],
      "reason": "The randomised message arms aim to separate T1 from T2, but the measure confounds message with product price point. The predictions ('higher WTP for premium when shown taste claims', 'higher WTP for low-cost when shown convenience claims') can both hold, so neither result clearly falsifies a theory. The decision rule is truncated, and the 'exceeds by ≥20%' comparison is ill-defined. The test relies on stated preference only. The Danish sample is not justified against the Flanders evidence. The cost and sample size are assumptions."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A16", "A15", "A17"],
      "reason": "A16 notes the simulated update and correctly avoids treating it as real demand. It then claims the update 'does not address T1' and leaves T2 at 'uncertain'. The what-if shows scheduled delivery plus reminders doubling 30-day enrollment (120 vs 60 of 400 per arm). That bears directly on T2's maintenance-convenience mechanism and on the 'subscription services for maintenance' value capture shared by T1. A16 also restates T1 verbatim and leaves the recommendation unchanged. Retention could have been justified on solvency or supply grounds, but the run never gives that reason."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A15", "A17"],
      "reason": "A17 commits to a next action (run A14) and states reversal conditions. However, it opens with 'Invest' in a premium health-enhancing proposition and rates T1 'likely' on one supporting excerpt. That contradicts the test-first next action. It also ignores the insolvency and financing constraint and the B2B/OEM route to market. The position is not supported by the evidence assessed."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A15", "A17"],
      "reason": "T1 is rated 'likely' and investment is recommended on the basis of one Flanders perception excerpt. The excerpt does not show willingness to pay or that a premium purifier would change behaviour. 'Health-enhancing' benefits are asserted as product facts without any source."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsound_revision",
      "event_ids": ["A16"],
      "reason": "The run dismisses the simulated update as irrelevant even though it bears on T2 and on the maintenance-subscription value capture. It gives no proportionate response and no explicit reason for retention."
    },
    {
      "kind": "non_discriminating_test",
      "event_ids": ["A14"],
      "reason": "The predictions for T1 and T2 are compatible with each other, message is confounded with price point, and the decision rule is incomplete. The test cannot cleanly separate the rivals."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "The constraints (€20,000 budget, 12-week timeline) have no source in the case or dossier. The run also omits the dossier's insolvency and financing constraint."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A11"],
      "reason": "Retrieved counter-evidence (aqp-srilanka-filter-disuse, aqp-dwd-recital40-confidence) is never reported or weighed. This amounts to selective citation."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "The episode is complete but has critical errors: an unsupported investment recommendation favouring T1, an unsound dismissal of the update evidence, and a proposed test that does not cleanly discriminate. Retrieved counter-evidence and the solvency constraint are ignored."
}

{
  "packet_id": "a56ebd74d4eec48b",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 lists three obvious alternatives: no expansion, consumer home laundry and niche professional. It also lists as a constraint 'No expansion into consumer markets beyond professional laundry', which presupposes the answer to the question being asked. It does not notice that consumer-operated and retail/wholesale channels already sit inside the professional offering. Nothing separates the objective from the suggested solution in a way that sharpens the question."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A4", "A5"],
      "reason": "The best theory (T1, A4) names benefits: convenience, premium pricing and diversification. Its behaviour-change claim, 'increasingly value convenience', is a generic assertion with no source. It does not explain who the buyer is: in consumer-operated setups the buyer is an operator, not the resident. It also does not explain why that buyer would pay a premium the manufacturer captures."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A4", "A5"],
      "reason": "T1 (self-service) and T2 (home laundry) nominally differ in product. However, they share near-identical mechanism text, identical assumptions ('Competitors are not strongly positioned...') and identical predictions ('Higher profit margins...', 'Expanded addressable market...'). They are rewordings of 'expand to consumers for premium pricing'. No theory represents the no-expansion or niche-professional alternatives listed in A1."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A4", "A5"],
      "reason": "Assumptions are stated but vague, such as 'demand ... is growing' and 'competitors are not strongly positioned'. Predictions like 'Increased sales' and 'Expanded addressable market' have no baseline, timeframe or threshold. They do not discriminate between T1 and T2."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A7"],
      "reason": "Sources are labelled company_assertion and some limits are noted. However, only agreeing readings are cited, and A7 misreads its source. The Compact Power excerpt explicitly says 'expand its addressable market within professional laundry'. It is offered as support for T2, a home/consumer proposition, which the quote's own wording undercuts. A6 does not note that consumer-operated setups are sold to operators, so they are not evidence of individual-consumer demand. Date, place and fit to the customer group are not addressed."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A8", "A10"],
      "reason": "The challenge searches (A8, A10) list returned sources but never report what was found or whether any of it contradicts the theories. epro-offering-professional-customers, which frames the Group as serving professional customers, is not engaged with. A8 and A10 also cite 'epro-selfservice-formats', which does not exist in the dossier."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9"],
      "reason": "The A9 test compares consumer-segment revenue against professional-segment revenue. It does not separate T1 (self-service) from T2 (home laundry). The T2 prediction is about the consumer proposition failing, not about home laundry, and the decision rule ('20% higher revenue') judges consumer expansion generally. The sample of 100, the cost and the threshold are assumptions. The test cannot discriminate between the stated rivals."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A12"],
      "reason": "The scheduled update was delivered and A12 responds to it, but unsoundly. SYN-D is explicitly simulated, concerns a warehouse label printer, and states 'Nothing about wearers or buyers was measured'. A12 nonetheless says it 'supports the need for a differentiated consumer proposition to capture premium pricing' and keeps T1 on that basis. This treats a simulated, irrelevant result as support."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "not_reached",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A13"],
      "reason": "A13 records that the investigation stopped before a recommendation because of the context limit. The interim position in A11 is internally contradictory: it says 'Favours T1' but argues against a consumer proposition. It also cites test results that never occurred. Unreached, so it contributes zero to the all-case composite."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "incomplete",
      "event_ids": ["A13"],
      "reason": "The episode stopped before a final recommendation (context limit)."
    },
    {
      "kind": "misleading_quote",
      "event_ids": ["A7"],
      "reason": "The exact quote says expansion is 'within professional laundry'. It is read as support for a consumer home-laundry proposition, which reverses the source's meaning."
    },
    {
      "kind": "invented_observation",
      "event_ids": ["A11"],
      "reason": "A11 claims 'evidence that the consumer market segment does not drive significant revenue growth'. The A9 test was only proposed and never run, and no source shows this."
    },
    {
      "kind": "invented_observation",
      "event_ids": ["A8", "A10"],
      "reason": "Both challenge searches cite the source 'epro-selfservice-formats', which is absent from the dossier."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A4", "A5", "A11"],
      "reason": "Growing demand, premium pricing, higher margins and weak competition are asserted with no source. A11 assigns T1 'very likely' while arguing for the opposite course."
    },
    {
      "kind": "non_discriminating_test",
      "event_ids": ["A9"],
      "reason": "The test compares consumer and professional revenue, not self-service against home laundry. Its outcomes cannot separate T1 from T2."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsound_revision",
      "event_ids": ["A12"],
      "reason": "A simulated, irrelevant label-printer result (SYN-D) is treated as real support for a consumer laundry proposition."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "The episode is incomplete: no recommendation was reached (A13). It also contains several critical errors: a misread quote (A7), invented test results (A11), a cited source not in the dossier (A8, A10), a non-discriminating test (A9), and an update based on simulated, irrelevant evidence (A12)."
}

{
  "packet_id": "7a140f4e2806b608",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 restates the question and lists options. Licensing (option 5) is a useful addition, but options 2 and 4 are the same option. The objective is not really separated from the suggested home-cook solution. A1 does not engage with the documented 1978 decision to concentrate solely on combi steamers (rational-wiki-1978) or with the company's stated professional-kitchen focus (rational-focus-quote). A1 also asserts a €20,000 budget and a 12-week timeline, which the case text does not supply."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9"],
      "reason": "T1 (A9) states who changes behaviour (health- and convenience-seeking home cooks) and why (marketing of time-saving, steam-based cooking). Value capture is generic: premium pricing, retail and subscriptions. It rests on an assumption that retail channels exist, and it is not tied to anything specific RATIONAL could capture over existing home combi-oven makers. It does not reach a full chain to value for this manufacturer."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 (A9) and T2 (A10) are near-rewordings. Both say home cooks will adopt a premium RATIONAL combi-steamer and differ only in the motivation that marketing emphasises. Structure, channels and predictions are almost identical. No theory represents the 'stay professional' side of the question, and none represents licensing or a no-demand explanation."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "The predictions are observable: purchase-intent lift at stated premiums, and stated reasons for purchase. The thresholds (15%, 10%, 20% premium) are arbitrary assumptions, not observations. These predictions test which positioning works, not whether residential demand exists at all. A failed lift would not seriously threaten the core claim that home cooks will adopt."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A13"],
      "reason": "Only rational-ir-customers is assessed (A11, A12). It is correctly labelled a company assertion that does not address home cooks. Highly relevant retrieved sources are never assessed:\n- rational-anova-oven shows a home countertop steam/convection combi oven already exists.\n- rational-anova-electrolux shows a home-appliance incumbent acquired Anova.\n- rational-combi-makers lists Gaggenau, Miele, AEG and Bosch as German combi makers.\n- rational-focus-quote states the company's professional focus.\nNone of these is used to judge competition or fit."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A15"],
      "reason": "A13 searches against T1 and retrieves rational-anova-oven and rational-anova-electrolux. These indicate existing home combi-oven competition and incumbent ownership. The findings are never reported, assessed or weighed. A15 then rates T1 'likely' as if nothing had been found."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A14"],
      "reason": "The A14 survey arms (convenience vs performance emphasis) could partly separate T1 from T2. The decision rule, however, only checks the T1 threshold and ignores T2. Stated purchase intent relative to a 'baseline without premium positioning' cannot establish absolute residential demand, willingness to pay, or advantage over existing home combi makers. It therefore cannot discriminate the actual decision between home entry and staying professional. Cost and sample are proposed assumptions."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "not_applicable",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": [],
      "reason": "Revision is excluded from packet.applicable_dimensions. No update was scheduled."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A15", "A16"],
      "reason": "A16 recommends developing a home-cook proposition and rates T1 'likely' with no supporting evidence. Its own uncertainty statement says demand is unknown. The 'would change if' clause is incoherent: meeting the 15-point threshold, which is the success condition in A14, would lead them to 'reconsider'. This inverts the decision rule. A next action is named, but the position is unsupported."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A15", "A16"],
      "reason": "T1 is rated 'likely' and home-cook development is recommended. No source supports residential demand, and the only assessed source (A11) is neutral."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A13"],
      "reason": "The challenge search retrieved evidence of existing home combi ovens and incumbent competition (rational-anova-oven, rational-anova-electrolux). It was silently ignored rather than reported."
    },
    {
      "kind": "non_discriminating_test",
      "event_ids": ["A14"],
      "reason": "The decision rule compares a positioning lift against a baseline using only the T1 arm. It cannot separate the home-entry option from the stay-professional option, and it ignores the T2 outcome."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A16", "A14"],
      "reason": "The reversal condition in A16 contradicts the decision rule in A14: reaching the success threshold triggers 'reconsider'."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "The €20,000 budget and 12-week timeline are stated as constraints but do not appear in the case text."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "The investigation is complete but has critical errors:\n- An unsupported favoured theory and recommendation (A15, A16).\n- Contrary challenge evidence that was retrieved and ignored (A13).\n- A test whose decision rule cannot settle the home-vs-professional choice (A14).\n- An internally contradictory reversal condition (A16)."
}

{
  "packet_id": "11fde27e5ea9dc58",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 states an objective and lists four obvious alternatives, including maintaining the current portfolio. It also notes that reach to individual buyers is unknown. It never challenges the question's premise or identifies a non-obvious constraint. It adds a 'test budget of €20,000 and 12-week timeline' that appears nowhere in the case or sources. That number is not grounded in the packet, which caps this dimension at 1."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10", "A11"],
      "reason": "Best theory T1 (A9) names a benefit: premium pricing for 'social features'. Its behaviour-change link rests on the CoffeeGeek quote (A11), which actually says home barista use 'can never offer' social interaction and that the cafe is the third place. So the stated mechanism is unsupported by its cited basis. The value-capture step ('charge higher prices for social features') is asserted, not traced."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 (social/community) and T2 (convenience) are rival explanations of home demand. They share an identical template and imply similar consumer products and retail channels, so they do not clearly imply different customers or channels."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "Predictions are observable: purchase intent versus a featureless machine, comparative sales, and average transaction value. They are loosely specified and partly generic across T1 and T2, for example the identical 'higher average transaction value'. No failure condition is stated that would clearly put either theory in trouble."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 0,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14"],
      "reason": "A11 labels a forum post (simo-coffeegeek-pandemic-2021) an 'independent_study' and reads it as support for T1, although the source says the opposite about home barista social value. A12 cites De'Longhi's 'rapid expansion into the prosumer market' as support for a convenience theory; the excerpt says nothing about convenience. The prosumer-slowdown source (simo-pdg-prosumer-slowdown-2024) and the post-pandemic decline source (simo-delonghi-q1-2023-decline) were retrieved but never assessed. No source is examined for date, place or fit to the customer group."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A14"],
      "reason": "A13 searches against T1, and A14 reasonably marks the Clingingsmith visible-goods study neutral. It fails to recognise that the CoffeeGeek source already retrieved contradicts T1. It never searches against T2 or engages the slowdown evidence. The challenge stays on safe ground."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A15"],
      "reason": "The conjoint survey has opposing predictions for T1 and T2 and can in principle separate them. The proposed €18,000 cost and 10-week duration are assumptions. It relies on stated preference among general panel consumers rather than the target enthusiasts. The decision rule is truncated ('If the difference is less.'), so its decision relevance is incomplete."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A17"],
      "reason": "A17 notes the delivered simulated update but responds unsoundly. It says the result 'demonstrates' that a social/learning mechanism drives purchases and supports T1. The source is simulated and explicitly does not test whether hobby or status mechanisms are true; it measures a skill-lesson offer, not social interaction. A17 conflates learning with social interaction and treats a what-if as demonstration."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "not_reached",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": ["A16", "A18"],
      "reason": "A18 says the investigation stopped before a recommendation because of repeated errors. A16 is an interim position only. It favours T1 as 'likely' and proposes a pilot on thin, misread evidence."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "incomplete",
      "event_ids": ["A18"],
      "reason": "The episode stopped before a completed recommendation."
    },
    {
      "kind": "misleading_quote",
      "event_ids": ["A11"],
      "reason": "The CoffeeGeek quote is clipped to 'The cafe is a true third place...' and used to support home baristas wanting social equipment. The full excerpt says being a home barista 'can never offer' social interaction, which reverses the meaning."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A12"],
      "reason": "A company statement about prosumer expansion is used as support for a convenience theory it does not address. It is also a marketing or company claim treated as evidence of demand."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A16"],
      "reason": "A16 rates T1 'likely' and recommends a social-feature pilot without valid supporting evidence."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsound_revision",
      "event_ids": ["A17"],
      "reason": "A17 treats a simulated lesson-offer result as demonstrating a social-interaction mechanism. The source disclaims this, and the inference conflates learning with social interaction."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A17"],
      "reason": "A simulated result is treated as real demand evidence ('demonstrates')."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "The €20,000 / 12-week budget constraint is not present in the case or sources and appears invented."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "Rejected. The episode is incomplete, with no recommendation (A18). It also contains a meaning-reversing quote (A11), unsupported evidence use (A12, A16), and an unsound update that treats simulated learning-offer data as proof of a social mechanism (A17). Any one of these is a critical error."
}

{
  "packet_id": "3469dfb73e4a2240",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A1", "A16"],
      "reason": "A1 restates the question as an objective and lists four overlapping alternatives. Alternative 3 mixes Simonelli's existing coffee-shop segment with retail consumers. The framing misses a false premise visible in the dossier: Victoria Arduino, a Simonelli brand, already sells a domestic flagship, the Eagle One Prima (simo-beanscene-prima-domestic, cited at A16). So the question is not purely 'beyond' professional. The budget and timeline constraints are not grounded in any source shown."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A11"],
      "reason": "T1 (A9) names a benefit and a generic value-capture route through premium pricing and DTC or specialty retail. Its behaviour-change step rests on an assertion about home 'social experience' that the cited source actually contradicts (A11). It does not trace how Simonelli specifically captures value given its existing channels or its Prima product."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T2 (A10) is incoherent as a rival. It claims coffee shops will be first adopters of a consumer product and that shop owners drive consumer sales. It largely restates T1's premium-demand idea through a 'professional halo' lens rather than offering a genuinely different explanation, customer or channel."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "The assumptions are stated, but the predictions are vague or conditional on launch, for example 'higher average transaction value than its current professional line'. That prediction is implausible as a test of T1 and would not put T1 in trouble if it failed. T2's prediction mixes shop owners and consumers incoherently."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 0,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14", "A15", "A16", "A18", "A19"],
      "reason": "A11 misreads the CoffeeGeek excerpt. It argues that home baristas cannot replace cafes' social role, which cuts against T1, yet it is cited as support and mislabelled 'independent_study'. A12 and A14 treat La Marzocco's prosumer expansion and the Breville–Lelit acquisition as support for a shop-driven theory without any link. A16 and A18 use the identical Prima quote to support both rivals. A15 applies a consumer-business decline to T2 rather than T1. The 'does not show' text is copied boilerplate. The source classes are mislabelled: news is called company_assertion and a forum comment is called a study. The revenue series and the 2021 product plan are never assessed."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
